1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,750
ROSIE: Colin, I'm really struggling to think of a topic for our human geography assignment.

2
00:00:04,750 --> 00:00:07,060
COLIN: Me too, Rosie. I'll tell you what

3
00:00:07,220 --> 00:00:10,271
let's think about the different aspects of human geography,

4
00:00:10,271 --> 00:00:14,630
and see if we can narrow the topic down a bit to help us decide.

5
00:00:14,630 --> 00:00:17,240
ROSIE: OK. So one aspect is population

6
00:00:17,700 --> 00:00:21,524
That would be all about population density and migration and so on.

7
00:00:21,524 --> 00:00:24,790
Lots of facts and statistics... Maybe a bit boring?

8
00:00:24,790 --> 00:00:28,460
COLIN: Yeah, but quite straightforward to find on the internet.

9
00:00:28,460 --> 00:00:33,337
ROSIE: Suppose so. How about health? I'd never thought about the links between that

10
00:00:33,337 --> 00:00:36,913
and geography until Professor Lee gave us that lecture on cholera.

11
00:00:36,913 --> 00:00:40,164
How in the nineteenth century a physician used street plans

12
00:00:40,164 --> 00:00:44,390
and plans of water supplies to find the source of a cholera epidemic.

13
00:00:44,390 --> 00:00:46,420
COLIN: Yes - fascinating, wasn't it

14
00:00:47,080 --> 00:00:51,240
Or we could do something more general - like economies.

15
00:00:51,240 --> 00:00:54,950
ROSIE: So how financial and commercial factors are linked to the physical environment?

16
00:00:54,950 --> 00:00:55,900
COLIN: Yeah.

17
00:00:55,900 --> 00:00:58,350
ROSIE: I thought that had been taken off the syllabus for this year.

18
00:00:58,350 --> 00:00:59,210
COLIN: Has it?

19
00:00:59,210 --> 00:01:01,760
ROSIE: I'm not sure. But it might be best to avoid it.

20
00:01:01,760 --> 00:01:05,780
COLIN: OK. Maybe we could do something on culture

21
00:01:06,240 --> 00:01:10,130
We had that lecture about culture and geography last week.

22
00:01:10,130 --> 00:01:11,640
ROSIE: I didn't get much out of that

23
00:01:11,960 --> 00:01:15,950
It was all so general and the lecturer didn't give any useful examples.

24
00:01:15,950 --> 00:01:19,740
COLIN: Yeah, I hardly took any notes, it didn't seem worth it.

25
00:01:19,740 --> 00:01:20,910
ROSIE: Me neither.

26
00:01:20,910 --> 00:01:25,300
COLIN: We could focus on poverty. That's something that's a global problem...

27
00:01:25,300 --> 00:01:29,427
ROSIE: The trouble is, Dr Lee was saying, that you have to be careful

28
00:01:29,427 --> 00:01:33,260
with some of the figures relating to poverty - they're sometimes deliberately manipulated.

29
00:01:33,260 --> 00:01:37,070
COLIN: You mean the information gets changed for political reasons?

30
00:01:37,070 --> 00:01:38,770
ROSIE: That sort of thing, yes.

31
00:01:38,770 --> 00:01:42,140
ROSIE: So what are we going to do our assignment on?

32
00:01:42,140 --> 00:01:46,735
I'll tell you what, one of the possibilities we haven't discussed is urbanisation.

33
00:01:46,735 --> 00:01:51,330
And now over half the world's population lives in cities, that's really important.

34
00:01:51,330 --> 00:01:54,210
COLIN: OK, good idea, Rosie. Let's do that.

35
00:01:54,210 --> 00:01:57,540
ROSIE: I love living in a big city, but of course there are problems.

36
00:01:57,540 --> 00:01:59,770
COLIN: Things like theft and robbery?

37
00:01:59,770 --> 00:02:01,618
ROSIE: Yes, but where I come from,

38
00:02:01,618 --> 00:02:03,940
that's linked to another more serious issue

39
00:02:04,320 --> 00:02:06,634
which is that a lot of people don't have jobs.

40
00:02:06,634 --> 00:02:08,218
That's getting worse and worse.

41
00:02:08,218 --> 00:02:11,800
And we also still have quite a lot of people who are homeless

42
00:02:11,940 --> 00:02:14,290
though that's not quite so bad as it was.

43
00:02:14,290 --> 00:02:17,940
COLIN: It'd be nice to talk about some positive developments.

44
00:02:17,940 --> 00:02:21,590
Like some of the new developments on the outskirts of cities.

45
00:02:21,590 --> 00:02:25,450
ROSIE: Yeah. They've opened some massive new shopping centres outside my city.

46
00:02:25,450 --> 00:02:26,920
COLIN: Yeah, the same with mine

47
00:02:27,200 --> 00:02:32,074
but it's meant a lot of the shops in the city centre are closing down.

48
00:02:32,074 --> 00:02:36,891
But the outskirts are ideal for buildings that need a lot of space, like for conferences.

49
00:02:36,891 --> 00:02:39,300
They've opened a couple of big ones.

50
00:02:39,300 --> 00:02:41,290
ROSIE: Yes, we've got some too.

51
00:02:41,290 --> 00:02:45,060
It'd be nicer to have more facilities for things like football too

52
00:02:45,440 --> 00:02:47,260
But that's not happening where I live.

53
00:02:47,260 --> 00:02:48,840
COLIN: Same in my area.

54
00:02:48,840 --> 00:02:51,951
ROSIE: We could include something about developing disused industrial sites.

55
00:02:51,951 --> 00:02:54,320
It seems like a good idea

56
00:02:54,340 --> 00:02:57,240
because you're not doing any harm to the natural environment.

57
00:02:57,240 --> 00:03:01,620
COLIN: Yeah, but aren't the buildings architecturally significant?

58
00:03:01,620 --> 00:03:06,477
ROSIE: Not really. And what people forget is that they often used quite dangerous materials -

59
00:03:06,477 --> 00:03:10,060
chemicals and things - which haven't been properly cleared away

60
00:03:10,360 --> 00:03:12,630
so the whole site has to be made safe.

61
00:03:12,630 --> 00:03:16,680
COLIN: Mm. That can't be cheap. And I bet it's often not budgeted for.

62
00:03:16,680 --> 00:03:17,790
ROSIE: You're right.

63
00:03:17,790 --> 00:03:19,970
COLIN: Have you read about Masdar City?

64
00:03:19,970 --> 00:03:24,704
ROSIE: In Abu Dhabi? Yes, it was designed to be a green city, wasn't it?

65
00:03:24,704 --> 00:03:26,780
That might be a good example of a city

66
00:03:26,780 --> 00:03:30,030
which set out to depend entirely on renewable energy.

67
00:03:30,030 --> 00:03:33,279
COLIN: Yes, we should say something about that.

68
00:03:33,279 --> 00:03:36,640
It was designed to be totally pedestrianised too

69
00:03:36,640 --> 00:03:39,370
wasn't it, with the transport underground.

70
00:03:39,370 --> 00:03:44,797
ROSIE: Yes, and they had big plans for recycling to reduce waste to the lowest possible level.

71
00:03:44,797 --> 00:03:47,670
But let's stick to talking about power sources.

72
00:03:47,670 --> 00:03:51,366
COLIN: Then there's that ecotown in England, Greenhill Abbots.

73
00:03:51,366 --> 00:03:56,171
It set out to conform to the usual principles - sustainability and so on.

74
00:03:56,171 --> 00:04:01,715
A lot of people were against it at first; they said the plans were unrealistic.

75
00:04:01,715 --> 00:04:06,890
I'm not sure how far they've got with it - I'll check.

76
00:04:06,890 --> 00:04:10,670
ROSIE: Right. So it looks as...
